as the onset of ideal magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) instabilities,
including kink (e.g. Török & Kliem 2005) and torus instabilities
(e.g. Kliem & Török 2006), and loss of equilibrium (e.g. Priest
& Forbes 2002).

The acceleration phase occurs when the erupting prominence
experiences a sudden increase in velocity. This phase is driven
either by magnetic reconnection taking place at the site of the
underlying current sheet (e.g. Lin & Forbes 2000; Karpen et al.
2012) or by an ideal MHD instability (e.g. Fan & Gibson 2007;
Démoulin & Aulanier 2010). After the acceleration phase, most
erupting filaments rise with an almost-constant velocity (e.g.
Kahler et al. 1988; Joshi & Srivastava 2007). This defines the
propagation phase. Different kinematic characteristics indicate
that different physical processes dominate in these phases. For
example, Lorentz forces are dominant during the acceleration
phase, while during the propagation phase the Lorentz force is
comparable to the gravitational and drag forces (Chen & Krall
2003; Temmer et al. 2011; Majumdar et al. 2020).

The initiation and acceleration phases of prominence erup-
tions close to the Sun are usually observed by EUV telescopes,
while the propagation phase of corresponding CMEs farther out
is usually covered by white-light coronagraphs (Mierla et al.
2013; Reva et al. 2017). Connecting low-corona EUV obser-
vations to high-corona white-light observations was attempted,
for example, by Byrne et al. (2014), D’Huys et al. (2017), and
O’Hara et al. (2019).

Since the launch of the Solar Orbiter mission (Müller et al.
2020), one of its remote-sensing instruments, the Extreme Ul-
traviolet Imager (EUI; Rochus et al. 2020), recorded numer-
ous erupting prominences with its Full Sun Imager (FSI), in
the 304 Å and 174 Å channels. A novel aspect of FSI that is
particularly important for studies of eruptive prominences is its
uniquely large (3.8◦ × 3.8◦) field of view (FOV), which allows
prominences to be tracked far out in the solar corona. In this Let-
ter, we report observations of a unique, large prominence erup-
tion that was observed on 15–16 February 2022 and tracked up
to heights above 6 R� in the 304 Å channel of FSI. This is the
first time that an erupting prominence emitting in EUV could be
tracked that far in the corona.

2. Observations

2.1. Kinematics

Positions of the observing spacecraft close to the prominence
eruption time (16 February 2022, 00:00 UT) are shown in Fig. 1.
The prominence observed by FSI in the 304 Å channel (FSI304)
was a bright and complex event with developed fine structure
(Fig. 2). The eruption started around 21:50 UT on 15 Febru-
ary, and the prominence was last seen in the FOV of FSI on
16 February around 05:00 UT. The eruption was first seen as
a bright prominence with a clear leading edge (LE) ahead of
it (Fig. 2a), resembling a typical three-part structure of a CME
(Illing & Hundhausen 1985). Around 01:40 UT on 16 February,
a trailing part of the erupting prominence was seen at heights as
great as 6.6 R� (the outer limit of the FSI FOV; see Fig. 2f). A de-
tailed description of the prominence eruption and the associated
flare is given in Appendix A.

The CME associated with the erupting prominence was
observed by the Large-Angle Spectroscopic COronagraph
(LASCO; Brueckner et al. 1995) aboard the Solar and Helio-
spheric Observatory (SOHO; Domingo et al. 1995) and by the
COR2 coronagraph (Howard et al. 2008) aboard the Solar–
TErrestrial RElations Observatory (STEREO A; Kaiser et al.

Fig. 1. Positions of Solar Orbiter, STEREO A, and Parker Solar Probe
with respect to Earth on 16 February 2022, 00:00 UT, in heliocentric
Earth equatorial coordinates. The black arrow shows the longitude of
the prominence eruption. The image was created using Solar-MACH
(https://solar-mach.github.io/).

2008) Ahead spacecraft. Figure 3 shows that the prominence
material constituting the core of the CME could be seen up to
∼7.96 R� from the Sun’s centre (the edge of the LASCO/C2
FOV).

To analyse the eruption kinematics, we tracked several off-
limb features (see Figs. 2 and 3) observed from at least two
vantage points to reconstruct their position in three-dimensional
(3D) space (Fig. 4). The overall tip of the prominence (i.e. the
CME core) was tracked at a position angle (PA) of ∼56◦ in both
FSI304 and COR1 data (the PA is measured anti-clockwise from
the north pole) and at a PA of ∼65◦ in the EUVI304 data. Three
bright knots of the prominence core (core1, core2, and core3) are
marked in Fig. 2b. The core1 feature is a bright knot at the lead-
ing part of the prominence (PA of ∼56◦) and was also observed
by the Extreme-UltraViolet Imager (EUVI) aboard STEREO A,
the Solar Ultraviolet Imager (SUVI), and the Atmospheric Imag-
ing Assembly (AIA). The core2 feature is a bright knot that
could be tracked at the PA of ∼60◦. The core3 feature is a kink-
like structure that was observed at the PA of ∼50◦, also seen
by LASCO C2 (PA of ∼46◦; see Fig. 3b), COR1 (PA of ∼51◦),
and COR2 (PA of ∼49◦). The three core features are sufficiently
compact for triangulation.

A concave feature was observed after 23:30 UT at a PA of
∼45◦. It was also observed by LASCO/C2 at a PA of ∼47◦ (see

